# Retire Typesense Debris

## Result

Run capture and session health no longer read or write Typesense. `memory/run.captured` appends metadata, chunks, and FTS rows to `~/.joelclaw/search/sessions.db`. The Typesense startup monitor now checks the Typesense process independently from SQLite session freshness.

Generic book search now resolves `docs_chunks` to `docs_chunks_v2`. Base document ingest no longer creates or writes the legacy `docs_chunks` collection. The v2 reindex function is the only chunk-index writer.

Commit `60d53337` deployed to Flagg and Blaine. A live capture canary reached SQLite in one second while `runs_dev` stayed at 107,682 documents.

## Deleted collections

- `runs_dev`: 107,682 documents
- `docs_chunks`: 17,101 documents
- `run_chunks_spike`: 3,199 documents
- `transcript_chunks_v1`: 60 documents
- `otel_events`: 0 documents

The verified export is at `~/.joelclaw/backups/typesense-retired/20260730T021238Z-first-cut`. Its manifest contains source counts and SHA-256 receipts. `runs_dev.full_text` was excluded because raw Run JSONL and `sessions.db` remain authoritative.

Typesense fell from 23 collections and 428,704 documents to 18 collections and 300,231 documents. Resident memory fell from 5.31 GiB before the cut to 1.34 GiB after deletion.

## Book collection isolation

The book move is complete.

- `com.joelclaw.typesense-books` owns loopback ports 8110 and 8109.
- The node contains exactly 662 `docs` and 241,939 `docs_chunks_v2`.
- Flagg and Blaine reach port 8110 through a tailnet-only TCP forward.
- docs-api reports both exact counts and routes through
  `DOCS_TYPESENSE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8110`.
- The CLI splits unified search across the book and operational nodes. Flagg and
  Blaine returned the same 9,014-match direct book canary and both returned
  mixed book/operational unified results.
- The system-bus worker restarted under its existing supervisor and
  re-registered 163 functions with book writers routed to port 8110.
- `docs` and `docs_chunks_v2` now return HTTP 404 on port 8108. They remain
  present with exact counts on port 8110.

The import exposed the actual Typesense failure. The book LaunchAgent inherited
a 256-file descriptor limit. At 260 open files, Typesense logged `Too many open
files`, lost its Raft leader, and returned no useful progress. The LaunchAgent
now sets `NumberOfFiles` to 65,536. After one 5 minute 34 second replay, the
resumable importer completed 1,301 batches with zero document errors.

The rollback export is
`~/.joelclaw/backups/typesense-books/20260730T053110Z`. The chunk JSONL contains
241,939 documents, is 3,276,496,486 bytes, and has SHA-256
`c00dd3fe67a067fa87a1475ab913376f19503f5664f85daca382cda6c3d8ccaf`.

## Still open

- Repair or retire stale `vault_notes`, `conversation_threads`,
  `pi_mono_artifacts`, and `slack_messages`.
- Remove `blog_posts`, `discoveries`, and `system_log` only after their remaining
  web/search callers move to canonical sources.
- Inspect duplicate v2 book chunks. The first post-cut query returned the same citation twice from distinct hits.

## Operational data verdict

After the book move, the operational node holds 16 collections and 58,113
documents. The collection list is no longer the main problem. Freshness and
field quality are.

Keep as live operational projections:

- `machines_dev`: 4 Machine enrollment mirrors. Capture auth no longer depends
  on it, but enrollment and registry sync still do.
- `gateway_behavior_history`: 84 records, current through July 26.
- `brain_graph_nodes`: 749 current graph nodes across the registered Brain
  roots.
- `system_knowledge`: 1,218 ADR, skill, insight, and turn-note records, current
  through July 21.
- `channel_messages`: 4,165 classified email, Slack, and Telegram messages,
  current through July 30. The repair updated 573 Slack timestamps from seconds
  to milliseconds without changing document IDs.
- `observations`: 801 compact session observations. It remains useful to recall,
  but it stops on July 20 and 34 legacy records have invalid 1970-era start
  values.

Keep as explicit bounded corpora, not pretend-live feeds:

- `slack_messages`: 33,262 records covering April 2025 through April 29, 2026.
- `pi_mono_artifacts`: 8,744 records covering the upstream repository through
  March 7.
- `vault_notes`: 4,308 records through April 16. Of these, 3,258 are old
  `log-entry` records and only 745 carry the newer `source` field.
- `blog_posts`, `discoveries`, `transcripts`, and `voice_transcripts`: 95 small
  searchable archive records combined. Their time metadata is inconsistent,
  but deleting them saves nothing material.
- `email_threads`: 37 narrow VIP thread records. This is a purpose-built view,
  not a general email index.

Fix or retire:

- `conversation_threads`: 2,159 derived threads. The repair normalized 73 Slack
  thread timestamps, but 2,147 records remain marked stale and only 12 are
  active.
- `system_log`: 2,487 records ending March 17. The live web syslog reads Convex,
  but generic search and old SDK adapters still query this frozen Typesense
  copy. Move those readers, export it, then delete the collection.

Replicas would not repair any of this. A Typesense replica duplicates the full
dataset and failure mode. The separate book process isolates the 241,939-chunk
library from capture and operational search, which is the boundary this system
needed.

## Deployment gate repair

The first cleanup commit exposed two existing Vercel-only failures.

- `apps/web` depended on `link:../../../joelclaw-api`, a sibling checkout that is absent in Vercel.
- The legacy Vercel Run routes pulled `bun:sqlite` into Next.js through `@joelclaw/memory`.

The web app now owns a checked public Convex contract in `apps/web/lib/convex-api.ts`. The Vercel Run ingest and search routes return `410`; Central system-bus owns Run ingest and SQLite owns search. The frozen lockfile install, web typecheck, and local production build pass.
